<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marib Dam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Great Dam of Marib</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib">Marib</a> Dam was an ancient dam across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-dhana">Wadi Adhanah</a> near Marib in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, built to capture seasonal floods and irrigate two large oases. Its earliest works date to the kingdom of Saba in the early 1st millennium BCE, and later rulers repeatedly repaired it, including the Himyarites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraha">Abraha</a> in the 6th century. Its final breach, around 570, is remembered in the Qur'an.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marib-dam.jpg" alt="Marib Dam" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H. Grobe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jemen1988-022_hg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The dam stood upstream, to the south-west, of the city of Maʾrib, capital of the Kingdom of Sabaʾ, a trading state that controlled the frankincense and spice routes. The Sabaeans built it to catch the periodic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> rains that fall on the nearby mountains. The earliest inscription on the dam records construction or repair under Yatha' Amar Watar I, who reigned in 760–740 BCE, and minor repairs followed under his successors.</p><p>In this form the dam was a bank of packed earth, triangular in cross section, 580 metres long and 4 metres high, joined to the rock on either side by stonework, with a spillway and sluices at its northern end. Around 500 BCE it was raised to 7 metres, its water face was covered with stone, and irrigation was extended to the southern side.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>After the Himyarites took control around 115 BCE they rebuilt it to a height of 14 metres, with five spillway channels, two masonry-reinforced sluices, a settling pond and a 1,000-metre canal to a distribution tank. These works, finished only in 325, irrigated about 100 square kilometres. The medieval geographer Yāqūt al-Ḥamawī described the water gathering behind the dam like a sea, to be let out through sluice gates as the crops needed it.</p><p>Silt building up behind the dam made upkeep ever more onerous. Major breaches are recorded in 449, 450, 542 and 548. Inscriptions on the dam give the costs and the large workforces of the repairs by Sharḥabīl Yaˁfar in 450 and by Abrahah in 543, and the last recorded repairs took place in 557.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a> (34:15–17) alludes to its destruction as the Flood of the ʿArim, sent upon Sabaʾ, whose two gardens were replaced with gardens of bitter fruit and tamarisks. Many scholars link that flood to a breach of about 145 BCE, during the war between the people of Raydān and Sabaʾ, but this remains uncertain, as migrations of the 2nd or 3rd centuries CE are also ascribed to the dam's failure. The collapse of the irrigation system provoked the migration of up to 50,000 people to other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>.</p><p>A new earth dam, 38 metres high and 763 metres long, was opened 3 kilometres upstream on 20 December 1986, built by Doğuş Group at the expense of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zayed-bin-sultan-al-nahyan">Sheikh Zayed</a> bin Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-nahyan">Al Nahyan</a>. In 2023 three locations across the ancient dam were added to the UNESCO World Heritage List with other landmarks of the Kingdom of Saba.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marib Dam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dams and diversion works, above all the <b>Marib Dam</b> inherited from Saba, watered the oases, and the kings took pride in maintaining them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At Marib, earthen and stone works diverted the floods of the Wadi Dhana into channels that watered two great oases, together covering several thousand hectares; these works were enlarged over many centuries into the famous <b>Marib Dam</b>, whose surviving sluice structures belong to later phases.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>His inscriptions record the restoration of the <b>Marib Dam</b> in 548 and a diplomatic conference attended by envoys of the Byzantine Empire, Persia and the Arab kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/aksumite-yemen">Aksumite Yemen</a></li></ul></section>
